==========================================
SYSADMIN DAY 02 NOTES — lsof
Date: 15-10-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- lsof lists every file (including network sockets) a process has open RIGHT NOW
- lsof -i shows network connections/ports; -sTCP:LISTEN narrows to just listening ports
- lsof -i :<port> and sudo fuser <port>/tcp both answer "what's using this port" - good for conflicts
- lsof -p <PID> / -c <name> / -u <user> filter by process, program name, or user
- FD column: cwd = working directory, txt = the program itself, a number like 3u = an open file (u=read+write)
- A deleted file stays on disk (and takes up space) until every process with it open closes it - lsof still shows it, marked (deleted)
- lsof +L1 finds exactly these "deleted but still open" files - fixes the classic "disk full but I can't find the file" problem

COMMANDS / ACTIONS I PRACTICED:
[ ] sudo lsof -i  /  -sTCP:LISTEN  /  -i :22
[ ] sudo lsof /var/log/syslog
[ ] sudo lsof -p <PID>  /  -c bash
[ ] sudo lsof -i :8080  vs  sudo fuser 8080/tcp (port conflict test)
[ ] the deleted-file-still-open trick (D1)

